WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.529
Like any good movie adapted from a comic book
with years of history, Aquaman had a few cool

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:08.520
Easter Eggs put there to reward eagle eyed
comics fans.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.370
From fun cameos to some pretty deep cuts that
only make sense if you've brushed up on your

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.520
comic book history, here are some of the Easter
Eggs that you may have missed in Aquaman.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.460
And beware: there be salty sea-spoilers ahead.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.300
It's no secret that tons of movies and TV
shows try to make themselves seem more real

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.010
by enlisting the help of real-world media
personalities to talk about the fictional

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:29.640
events or characters you're watching.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.020
You know the moments - like when Anderson
Cooper appeared on CNN to cover the big superhero

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.950
throwdown in Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.980
But Aquaman goes in a different direction.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.300
Just as folks in the DCEU read the Daily Planet
rather than The New York Times, they also

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.300
watch other channels instead of, say, MSNBC.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.980
When the characters in Aquaman flip on the
news, the anchors sit behind the desk at GBS,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.800
a fictional network pulled straight from the
pages of DC Comics.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.540
Longtime comics readers might know that in
1971's Superman #233, Clark Kent gave TV a

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.500
shot, working as a news anchor for WGBS.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.020
Pretty much all of DC Comics' superheroes
have their own fictional locales where they

00:01:09.021 --> 00:01:11.260
hang their capes, and Aquaman's no different.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.580
The battle for Atlantis' throne is one of
the film's central plotlines.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.010
However, while we had no shortage of undersea
civilizations to see as the movie progressed,

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.820
another scene in the movie seemed to lay the
foundation for a different superhero who we

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.880
haven't actually met in this version of the
DC Extended Universe.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.200
During the one and only credits-scene, we
see a clipping from a newspaper asking about

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.320
the identity of Aquaman - and the newspaper
itself is the Coast City Gazette, presumably

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.370
the hometown paper for another important DC
superhero, Green Lantern.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:43.870
Sure, if you saw the Ryan Reynolds-starring
Green Lantern movie from 2011, you're probably

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:48.170
already familiar with Coast City… but there's
actually a pretty good chance you didn't see

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.510
it, since it didn't sell enough tickets to
justify a sequel.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.080
"Please don't make the supersuit green.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.340
Or animated."

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.880
Since Warner Bros. rebooted DC's cinematic
universe after Green Lantern bombed, we've

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:04.490
been introduced to tons of heroes and villains,
but except for a small cameo during a flashback

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.840
scene in Justice League, Green Lantern is
still MIA from this incarnation of DC's movieverse.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.700
Could this small newspaper clipping lay the
groundwork for Green Lantern's impending return?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.720
Most DC Comics fans know that Aquaman has
a sidekick who usually goes by the name Aqualad.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.260
But even though Aqualad has had comic book
and animated adventures of his own in the

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.110
Teen Titans and the Team from Young Justice,
Aquaman has yet another sidekick who rarely

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.420
gets the respect he deserves.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.700
That's right, we're talking about Topo the
octopus, an eight-armed sentinel of justice

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.810
who Aquaman taught to play several instruments
at once.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.160
Mostly so he could play "Happy Birthday" should
the need arise.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:41.840
No, really.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.460
As silly as the Aquaman movie turned out to
be, James Wan and the rest of the filmmakers

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:50.960
wisely decided not to go so far as to have
Arthur hang out with his octopus pal.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.680
However, Wan has, indeed, confirmed that Topo
makes an appearance in the movie anyway.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.910
He's not easy to miss: he's the giant octopus
playing the drums during the Ring of Fire

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.220
battle scene between Arthur and King Orm.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.480
For a movie set in the DC Comics' multiverse,
there weren't a ton of overt nods to comics

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:10.190
themselves… or at least, that's how it might
seem.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.239
However, there's a distinct possibility that
one of the movie's key locations - the sea

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:17.960
in the center of the Earth - could actually
be one of the most obscure Easter Eggs you

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:18.700
could imagine.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.120
In the mid-1970s, DC Comics published a series
called Warlord, about an Air Force pilot who

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:29.320
crash lands in a place called Skartaris, a
lush jungle land filled with fantastic creatures

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.320
and dinosaurs.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.849
And, in the third act of the movie, Aquaman
and Mera find themselves in a mysterious land

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:39.129
in the center of the Earth, filled with lush
jungle and dinosaurs, where they're reunited

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:42.140
with Atlanna, who's been there by herself
for 20 years.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:46.709
While Skartaris in the comics does, in fact,
feature various factions of people, Atlanna

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:48.980
said she was alone the entire time she was
there.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:53.900
That might mean that wherever she'd been might
not actually be Skartaris, or simply that

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.660
she never managed to encounter any of the
land's inhabitants.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.120
Maybe Warner Bros. will announce a Warlord
adaptation coming to the big screen any day

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:01.620
now.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.200
But until they do, this Easter Egg might not
be as fresh as the others.

